# Code Quality and Style Rules

Follow existing project style.

If no style exists:

- choose simple readable conventions
- prefer clarity over cleverness
- use meaningful names
- keep functions small
- keep modules focused
- avoid excessive abstraction

## General Rules

- Use type annotations where supported.
- Validate inputs.
- Handle errors explicitly.
- Avoid magic constants.
- Avoid duplicated code.
- Avoid unnecessary dependencies.
- Use configuration instead of hardcoding.
- Write code that can be tested.
- Keep comments useful and not noisy.

## Error Handling

Errors should be:

- explicit
- actionable
- logged when appropriate
- not leaking secrets
- not hiding root cause from logs
- user-safe in API/UI responses

## Logging

Use structured logs where possible.

Include relevant IDs:
- request ID
- user/session ID if applicable
- job ID
- resource ID
- source ID
- trace ID

Do not log secrets or sensitive raw data.

## Refactoring

Refactor only when needed for the requested task.

Do not perform broad unrelated refactors.
